inference_output.content:
Below is a **complete, ready‑to‑paste example** that you can drop into any web page (or into the HTML/JavaScript section of most mapping‑software platforms that support custom code).  
It creates a Google Map, adds the three markers you asked for, draws the polygon you over‑laid earlier, and sets the polygon’s opacity to 50 % – exactly the same view you now have in the “GlobalMap” you’ve been working with.

---

## 1️⃣  What you’ll need

| Item | How to get it |
|------|---------------|
| **Google Maps JavaScript API key** | Go to the Google Cloud Console → **APIs & Services → Credentials** → **Create API key**. Make sure the **Maps JavaScript API** and **Maps Static API** are enabled for that key. |
| **A place to host the HTML/JS** | Any web server, local file (`file://…` works for testing), or the “custom HTML/JS” widget of your mapping software. |

---

## 2️⃣  Full HTML + JavaScript snippet

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>GlobalMap – Custom Markers & Polygon</title>
  <style>
    /* Full‑screen map – adjust as you like */
    #map {
      height: 100vh;
      width: 100%;
    }
  </style>
  <!-- 1️⃣  Load the Google Maps JavaScript API (replace YOUR_API_KEY) -->
  <script src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key=YOUR_API_KEY"></script>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Initialise the map – centre it roughly between the points
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById('map'), {
    zoom: 5,                     // you can change this
    center: { lat: 45, lng: 7 }, // a rough centre (between Paris & Rome)
    mapTypeId: 'roadmap'         // or 'satellite', 'terrain', etc.
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Helper to add a marker with a title (info‑window)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  function addMarker(lat, lng, title) {
    const marker = new google.maps.Marker({
      position: { lat, lng },
      map,
      title
    });

    const info = new google.maps.InfoWindow({
      content: `<strong>${title}</strong><br>Lat: ${lat}<br>Lng: ${lng}`
    });

    marker.addListener('click', () => info.open(map, marker));
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add the three markers you requested
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // Statue of Liberty – Northwest (Paris‑ish coordinates)
  addMarker(48.8566, 2.3522, 'Statue of Liberty - Northwest');

  // Statue of Liberty – Southeast (Rome‑ish coordinates)
  addMarker(41.9028, 12.4964, 'Statue of Liberty - Southeast');

  // San Francisco (the last point you added)
  addMarker(37.7749, 122.4194, 'San Francisco');

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Draw the polygon (the same two points you gave us)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const polygon = new google.maps.Polygon({
    paths: [
      { lat: 48.8566, lng: 2.3522 },   // point 1
      { lat: 41.9028, lng: 12.4964 }   // point 2
    ],
    strokeColor: '#FF0000',
    strokeOpacity: 0.8,
    strokeWeight: 2,
    fillColor: '#FF0000',
    fillOpacity: 0.5,   // 50 % transparency as you requested
    map
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6️⃣  OPTIONAL: Fit the map bounds to show everything nicely
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const bounds = new google.maps.LatLngBounds();
  bounds.extend(new google.maps.LatLng(48.8566, 2.3522));
  bounds.extend(new google.maps.LatLng(41.9028, 12.4964));
  bounds.extend(new google.maps.LatLng(37.7749, 122.4194));
  map.fitBounds(bounds);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How to use it

1. **Replace `YOUR_API_KEY`** with the key you generated in step 1.  
2. Save the file as `globalmap.html` (or any name you like).  
3. Open it in a browser – you should see the map with the three markers and the semi‑transparent polygon.  
4. **If your mapping software accepts an “HTML widget” or “custom script”**, paste the whole `<script>` block (or the entire file) into that widget. Most GIS platforms (ArcGIS Online, QGIS Web Client, Mapbox GL JS, etc.) let you embed a Google Map this way.

---

## 3️⃣  What if you need a **static image** instead?

If your software only accepts a static picture (e.g., a PDF report, a dashboard that can’t run JavaScript), you can generate one with the same data:

```javascript
// Example using the helper function `generateStaticMap` (you can call it via the tool you have)
generateStaticMap({
  center: "45,7",          // centre of the view
  size: "800x600",
  zoom: 5
});
```

Then overlay the markers/polygon manually in an image editor, or use the **Google Static Maps API** with `markers=` and `path=` parameters. The URL would look like:

```
https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/staticmap?
center=45,7&zoom=5&size=800x600
&markers=color:red%7Clabel:N%7C48.8566,2.3522
&markers=color:blue%7Clabel:S%7C41.9028,12.4964
&markers=color:green%7Clabel:F%7C37.7749,122.4194
&path=fillcolor:0xFF000080|weight:2|48.8566,2.3522|41.9028,12.4964
&key=YOUR_API_KEY
```

(Replace `YOUR_API_KEY`.)

---

## 4️⃣  Quick checklist

- [ ] **API key** added.  
- [ ] **HTML/JS** placed in a page or widget that your software can render.  
- [ ] Markers appear with the correct titles.  
- [ ] Polygon is visible and semi‑transparent.  
- [ ] (Optional) Adjust `zoom`, `center`, or `mapTypeId` to suit your layout.

---

### Need anything else?

- **Different map style** (satellite, dark mode, custom JSON style) – just change `mapTypeId` or use `map.setOptions({styles: …})`.  
- **Export to KML/GeoJSON** for use in other GIS tools – let me know and I can generate that for you.  
- **Integration with a specific platform** (ArcGIS, QGIS, Mapbox, etc.) – I can tailor the code to that environment.

Just tell me what you’d like to tweak, and I’ll help you get it in place!

